A trivially copyable label, so the interpolation union can be relocated

`union Data` holds a `label` and lives in a `vector<Data>`, which has to relocate its
elements when it grows. A union whose variant member has a non-trivial copy constructor
has its own copy constructor deleted, and `label` was a `std::pair`.

libc++ gives `pair` a `__non_trivially_copyable_base`, so it is not trivially copyable
there even when both members are; libstdc++ has no such base, which is why this builds
elsewhere. A two-field aggregate with the comparison `map` needs has no such problem.

`label` is local to this file and only ever aggregate-initialised or used as a map key, so
nothing reads `.first` or `.second`.
---
diff --git a/soh/soh/frame_interpolation.cpp b/soh/soh/frame_interpolation.cpp
index ae2ee0e..13a2f53 100644
--- a/soh/soh/frame_interpolation.cpp
+++ b/soh/soh/frame_interpolation.cpp
@@ -105,7 +105,17 @@ enum class Op {
     SkinMatrixMtxFToMtx
 };
 
-typedef pair<const void*, int> label;
+// A plain aggregate rather than a std::pair, because it is a member of the union below and that
+// union has to be trivially copyable for `vector<Data>` to relocate on growth. libc++'s pair
+// carries a non-trivially-copyable base, which deletes the union's copy constructor.
+struct label {
+    const void* ptr;
+    int idx;
+
+    bool operator<(const label& o) const {
+        return ptr != o.ptr ? ptr < o.ptr : idx < o.idx;
+    }
+};
 
 union Data {
     Data() {
